import{_ as t,o,c as a,a2 as s}from"./chunks/framework.1C62pOns.js";const n="/pro-agent-hub-installer/capturas/en/mcp-externo.png",r="/pro-agent-hub-installer/capturas/en/mcp-proprio.png",m=JSON.parse('{"title":"MCP services","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"en/interface/mcp.md","filePath":"en/interface/mcp.md","lastUpdated":1791311821000}'),i={name:"en/interface/mcp.md"};function h(l,e,c,d,p,u){return o(),a("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[s('<h1 id="mcp-services" tabindex="-1">MCP services <a class="header-anchor" href="#mcp-services" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;MCP services&quot;">​</a></h1><p><strong>MCP</strong> (Model Context Protocol) is the standard by which an agent gains tools that did not come built in. This screen registers two rather different kinds of record, and the choice is made at creation time:</p><ul><li><strong>External</strong> — a third party&#39;s MCP server, which you consume by URL.</li><li><strong>Own</strong> — a server <em>you</em> write here, and the platform publishes.</li></ul><h2 id="external" tabindex="-1">External <a class="header-anchor" href="#external" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;External&quot;">​</a></h2><p><img src="'+n+'" alt="mcp-externo"></p><p>All it takes is the transport, the URL and the timeout. Nothing runs on your installation — the platform simply talks to the address you gave.</p><p><strong>Forward the signed-in user&#39;s token</strong> starts off, and should stay off in almost every case. Turned on, the platform sends the server the credential of whoever is talking to the agent — which lets that server act as that user on the platform itself. It only makes sense for a server under your control that needs to know who called; never for a third party&#39;s service.</p><p>Someone whose group grants only <strong>edit</strong> can change how the server is used (description, timeout, status), but <strong>not</strong> the address, the transport or the user-token forwarding — those are master&#39;s. The reason: pointing the URL at an address of their own, with forwarding on, would hand that address the credential of every user who talks to the agent.</p><p>Once saved, link the service to an agent on the agent&#39;s Tools tab.</p><h2 id="own" tabindex="-1">Own <a class="header-anchor" href="#own" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Own&quot;">​</a></h2><p><img src="'+r+'" alt="mcp-proprio"></p><p>Here you write the tools in Python and the platform brings up an MCP server for them, in a separate process — a slow MCP service never ties up an API request.</p><h3 id="tools-tab" tabindex="-1">Tools tab <a class="header-anchor" href="#tools-tab" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Tools tab&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Each top-level function becomes a tool. <strong>Write no decorator and no route</strong>: the platform wraps the functions and assembles <code>register_tools</code> itself. The name, the signature and the docstring are the contract the model reads to decide when to call it.</p><p>The scope carries <code>log()</code> and the data, system and integration operations — see <a href="/pro-agent-hub-installer/en/funcoes/mcp_tools">Own MCP</a>.</p><p>To find out <strong>who</strong> is calling, use <code>connected_user()</code>: it returns the name, e-mail, phone number, groups and whether the user is a master — or <code>None</code> when the caller was an integration rather than a person. Use it when the answer depends on the person (their own accounts, what they may see) instead of asking for the name as a tool parameter: parameters are filled in by the model, and it can fill them with any name. The identity is only trustworthy with authentication turned on — see the Authentication tab.</p><h3 id="helpers-tab" tabindex="-1">Helpers tab <a class="header-anchor" href="#helpers-tab" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Helpers tab&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Support code: <code>import</code> statements, constants and shared functions. <strong>Nothing here becomes a tool</strong>, even a top-level function. It is the line between &quot;what the model may call&quot; and &quot;what your code uses internally&quot;.</p><h3 id="authentication-tab" tabindex="-1">Authentication tab <a class="header-anchor" href="#authentication-tab" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Authentication tab&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Ahead of the three keys there is a <strong>master switch</strong>: the toggle that turns the service&#39;s authentication on. With it off, none of the others is consulted — the published service accepts any call.</p><div class="danger custom-block"><p class="custom-block-title">Turning authentication off opens the service to whoever reaches the port</p><p>A new service is created with the master switch <strong>on</strong>, accepting the signed-in user and the workflow token — the installation&#39;s own agents and workflows call it with no configuration at all. Turning it off is an explicit choice, and a big one: an own service is published on the installation&#39;s domain, at <code>/mcp/&lt;port&gt;/</code>, so its tools — which are Python executed on the server, with access to the registered database connections, the agents and the integrations — are within reach of anyone who gets to that address, with no credential at all.</p><p>Services created <strong>before version 1.0.26.6</strong> were born with the master switch off and stay that way: the upgrade changes the state of no existing service. Check the Authentication tab of each one.</p><p>With authentication off, <code>connected_user()</code> is worthless too: with no authentication the platform only has the name the caller itself wrote in the header, and it returns the matching record with <code>verified: False</code>. Never decide a permission inside a tool without checking that field.</p></div><p>The three keys, and what each one opens:</p><ul><li><strong>Fixed token</strong> — a secret the caller presents. This is what you hand to a client outside the platform.</li><li><strong>Accept workflow token</strong> — lets workflows and planners of the installation itself call the service without a token of their own.</li><li><strong>Accept user authentication</strong> — lets a signed-in user call it, respecting their groups.</li></ul><p>With authentication on and all three off, the platform <strong>generates a fixed token</strong> and shows it on screen: the service is not left unreachable, it is left reachable only by whoever holds that token.</p><h3 id="versions-tab" tabindex="-1">Versions tab <a class="header-anchor" href="#versions-tab" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Versions tab&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Every save keeps a version of the code. You can compare, restore and leave a note on each one — the history is what lets you touch a service already in use without fear.</p><h3 id="publishing" tabindex="-1">Publishing <a class="header-anchor" href="#publishing" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Publishing&quot;">​</a></h3><p>An own service only goes live once published, and it runs on the <strong>port</strong> on its record, from 8003 to 8099. Each service has its own.</p><p>The screen shows two addresses:</p><ul><li><strong>URL</strong> — the internal address, <code>http://pro_ai_mcp:&lt;port&gt;/mcp</code> (or <code>/sse</code>). This is what the installation&#39;s agents and workflows use.</li><li><strong>External address</strong> — <code>https://&lt;your domain&gt;/mcp/&lt;port&gt;/mcp</code> (or <code>/sse</code>), served by nginx with TLS. This is what you paste into an MCP client outside the platform: Claude Desktop, Cursor, n8n. It appears when the installation sets <code>PRO_AI_APP_PUBLIC_URL</code>.</li></ul><div class="info custom-block"><p class="custom-block-title">Changed in version 1.0.26.7</p><p>Up to 1.0.26.6 a service was reached from outside through the server&#39;s port (<code>http://&lt;server&gt;:8003/mcp</code>), and only ports 8003 to 8006 were published. Those ports are no longer published: switch the address in external clients to the screen&#39;s <strong>External address</strong>.</p></div><div class="warning custom-block"><p class="custom-block-title">Publishing is reconciled, not instantaneous</p><p>The process serving the MCPs periodically compares what is live with what is in the database. After publishing, the endpoint appears within seconds — not at the same instant as the click.</p></div><p><strong>Test</strong> calls the tool straight from the screen, with no agent involved. It is how you tell &quot;the tool is wrong&quot; apart from &quot;the model did not choose it&quot;.</p>',33)])])}const w=t(i,[["render",h]]);export{m as __pageData,w as default};
